# Packet #13 — "Fresh Guacamole" by PES

## 1. Specimen identity + technical facts
- **Title:** Fresh Guacamole by PES | Oscar Nominated Short (platform title)
- **Creator:** PES (Adam Pesapane) · **Platform:** YouTube (channel: PES) · **URL:** https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dNJdJIwCF_Y
- **Uploaded:** 2013-03-07 · **Duration:** 100.894 s · **Format:** 1920×1080 @ 24 fps, VP9 + Opus, 37,333,954 B archived as source.mp4 (probe.json)
- **Displayed counts at capture (post.json, copied not interpreted):** 495,478,875 views · 3,119,792 likes · 80,000 comments. Caption (creator's description) identifies it as the 2013 Academy Award–nominated film and the 2nd in PES's Food Film Trilogy.
- **Speech record:** no platform subtitles or auto-captions exist for this upload; a full-track ASR/VAD census (faster-whisper base, VAD filter) returned **0 speech segments** in 100.894 s (asr-segments.json). The film is music + performed sound effects only.
- **Evidence on disk:** 101-frame 1 fps census (frames/) · 29 evidence frames (ev/) · 7 clips (clips/) · scene census 31 candidate boundaries @0.35 / 83 @0.20 (distinct pts_time; true shot count UNVERIFIED — threshold sensitivity, see §4).

## 2. Consumer + production surface
**Primary consumer: the DLC assembler — transition containers.** This packet's surface is the **substitution-at-action transition**: a hidden (or action-covered) swap in which the object changes identity inside a performed action, and the action itself assigns the new identity. The assembler's transition containers use this packet in their Observe by building FROM §3's mechanism entries — each entry records the visible substitution separately from the inferred edit (before state · after state · spanning action · whether a shot boundary is visible), so a container can be built from the evidence fields rather than from an impression of the trick.
**Secondary consumer: Hadosh Academy explainer production — diagram and word-animation surfaces**, for object-metaphor substitution: an object cast in one role is re-read by the verb performed on it (casting vs literalization, §3 T07/T09), the mechanism an explainer needs when a diagram object must change meaning mid-argument without a cutaway.

## 3. Technique catalogue
_Evidence form (A4 cl.3): a frame binds visual claims; a clip binds sequence/timing claims; the ASR/VAD census binds the speech claim in §1. Frame citations are local-disk evidence (the packet's bulk media does not travel with the synced record)._

### T01 — Bookend title cards (author mark as frame)
- **Timestamps:** 0–4 s (PES card, title card) · 95–100.9 s (end card, PES card)
- **Mechanics:** Black field, single line of green type ("FRESH GUACAMOLE / BY PES"), held in silence (the 0–4.45 s near-silent window) before any object appears; the same card pair closes the film in reverse order. Nothing moves inside the cards — the bookend works by stillness against the dense action it frames.
- **Why it works:** the cards price the film as an authored object before the first gag, and their return at the end converts a sequence of tricks into a signed piece.
- **Evidence:** ev/t001-pes-card.jpg (the opening PES author card) · ev/t002-title-card.jpg (the opening title card) · ev/t093-end-card.jpg (the closing title card) · ev/t099-pes-card-end.jpg (the closing PES author card)

### T02 — Tool-preparation ritual as opening beat
- **Evidence:** ev/t022-baseball-peel.jpg (baseball skin peeled back, white core in hand)

### T08 — Slice integrity: cuts that hold together
- **Timestamps:** 24–29 s (S3)
- **Mechanics:** The white baseball is set on the board and sliced; the slices separate slightly but hold their arrangement, their red stitching now reading as onion-ring patterning. The knife work is ordinary; the reading is done by the stitching's geometry.
- **Why it works:** a restraint beat — not every action transforms; holding the object stable here makes the next beat's transformation legible by contrast.
- **Evidence:** ev/t027-baseball-slice.jpg (knife through the baseball on the board, slices forming)

### T09 — Verb literalization: dicing produces dice
- **Timestamps:** 29–36 s (S3) · **Clip:** clips/baseball-dicing.mp4 (28–36 s)
- **Mechanics:** The slices are gathered and — at the moment the knife begins to dice them — they are a small stack of white dice. Successive chops multiply the dice: each pass lands more, smaller dice, until the board carries a pile of tiny dice, which the hands gather like chopped onion. Two mechanisms are coded separately (P-A4): the initial CASTING (baseball as onion, T07–T08) and the LITERALIZATION — the verb "to dice" made visible as its homonym. The multiplication is an intra-shot change: visible object-state changes with no shot boundary claimed.
- **Why it works:** the pun is executed as procedure, not caption — the viewer watches the word happen, which is why it survives translation to any language.
- **Evidence:** ev/t032-dice-stack.jpg (the slices landed as a stack of dice under the knife) · ev/t035-tiny-dice.jpg (the multiplied pile of tiny dice on the board) · ev/t038-dice-sprinkle.jpg (the tiny dice sprinkled into the molcajete over the green flesh)

### T10 — Tool transformation: pincushion tomato through the grid press
- **Timestamps:** 39–46 s (S4) · **Clip:** clips/pincushion-grid.mp4 (41–46 s)
- **Mechanics:** A tomato-red pincushion is de-pinned (its pins read as the tomato's stem-points), set into a grid cutter, and pressed through; what emerges is a slab of translucent red cubes — diced tomato — gathered by hand and added to the molcajete. The transformation is performed by a real kitchen tool doing its real job; the substitution rides inside the press's occlusion.
- **Why it works:** the tool's function is the argument — a grid cutter can only output cubes, so the output's identity is forced by the instrument, not by the object's resemblance.
- **Evidence:** ev/t041-pincushion-tomato.jpg (pincushion in hand, pins visible) · ev/t044-grid-press.jpg (the grid cutter pressing the pincushion)

### T11 — Halving reveal + press: the golf-ball lime
12:- **Empty-pool form (promoted 2026-11-15, Cycle 010 Condense — candidate filed in Cycle 009's Condense; promotion condition was a second empty firing demonstrating the same shape, and Wave 8 is that firing; clause wording derives from the Cycle-009 candidate):** a wave with zero owed first-attempt items is a lawful cycle, not a skipped one. Its product is the finding form: (1) a pool census priced from the QUEUE recreation section, the catalogue register (TECHNIQUES.md + INDEX.md), the intake directory, and a binding artifact census (per-directory scores.md + codex/ return presence), plus board hand-offs where intake is pending; (2) the cycle-level guard firings — codex-observe in its cycle-level form, jev-observe-exit, jev-plan-exit, codex-condense — fired unconditionally, with per-item/per-build calls (codex-plan-review · jev-prewave · the five-lens scoring wave) printed NOT-DEMANDED-BY-FORM with their reasons; (3) the filings (HQ.json entry · verdict-history no-verdict line · lane heartbeat beat); (4) upstream intake state priced in the open (what is built, what stands unregistered/unadopted, which job owns the next act) — an empty pool here is never printed as an empty lane upstream, and the next wave's Observe re-censuses fresh rather than inheriting the finding. No item is invented, no practice re-attempt is misrouted, and no verdict is counterfeited for an unbuilt wave. First demonstration: Cycle 009 Wave 7 (2026-11-14). Second demonstration + this promotion: Cycle 010 Wave 8 (2026-11-15).
18:2. Read the original segment in motion: the archived source at the catalogue timestamps (the cut is made in §3, but Observe watches the segment in the source first and notes the sub-beats: what forms when, holds how long, dissolves how).
22:- **RESOURCE LIST (read in this order, every wave):** (1) `../QUEUE.md` recreation-queue section — current positions, OWED status, ownership split (first attempts here; scored techniques route to practice) · (2) the item's packet section in its digest.md + TECHNIQUES.md entry (style-library) · (3) the item's prior scores.md + codex/ returns + `verdict-history.md` lines, including named family siblings (e.g. T16 reads T15's filings) · (4) the evidence frames + archived source segment in motion · (5) this file's Amendments A1/A3/A5 guidance for the item's family (flow-field model + Owner Cures 1–2 for particle fields; runtime text sampling for typography) · (6) `guards/guards.md` command list.
50:4. **Codex scoring wave (external):** FIVE reviewers, distinct lenses — **motion fidelity · timing/tempo match · construction purity · reusability/parameterization · readability** (the fifth lens is permanent since 2026-11-14, Amendment A5: formed-word legibility against the particle field — word-vs-reserve intensity separation present and sufficient · the word readable at phone size during its hold · background particles never competing at word intensity). Each run: instruction file opens with the standing header (consumer + the decision the run prices; the working record it builds from and writes to; the return must name what it wrote into the body), carries the technique's catalogue mechanics + the recreation's full source, and returns ONE lens score (0–100) + priced findings. Feeding follows the fleet's Codex rule (instructions + artifact in ONE stream; never pipe + stdin field together). Returns are filed verbatim under the artifact's `codex/` directory.
69:1. **Convergent-lens rule (§5 reading rule):** when two or more lenses independently name the same defect, that defect is priced as the next attempt's primary cure, ahead of single-lens findings. (T15 attempt 1: motion 68 + reuse 64 both named the surplus-particle freeze during FORM.)
70:2. **Typography construction precedent:** runtime sampling of config-rendered text is the lawful letterform construction (purity 100, T15 attempt 1) — future typography recreations start from it rather than re-deriving purity.
76:1. **The queue orders; it never exempts.** If a catalogued lesson is a motion graphic that can be recreated as HTML, its recreation MUST be created. Triage survives only as ordering + pacing. Classification rule, applied per technique at registration and printed in the open: (a) is it a motion graphic (its lesson is motion/animation behavior, not a still, a system, or a story structure)? (b) can HTML/CSS/JS + generated assets carry that motion? Yes+yes = **OWED**. A no on either leg is priced with its reason in QUEUE.md and the tier register (T24 static end card: no motion content — documentation asset; T25 two-world system: a design system whose component techniques are individually owed — not itself one recreation).
77:2. **Tier accounting consequence:** a motion-graphic technique at DESCRIBED prints as **OWED + its queue position** (style-library Amendment A3 enforces the face).
78:3. **Division of labor with the practice job:** this job owns FIRST attempts (unattempted techniques, queue order). Re-attempts of already-scored recreations belong to the practice job (lowest-scored first). A technique mid-attempt here finishes its verdict here before practice can pick it up.
107:## Amendment A9 (2026-11-16, Cycle 023 Condense — the five Cycle-022 NOT-YET-AMENDED landings, plus this cycle's pattern promotion)
109:1. **Plate construction is a Plan requirement (A9.1).** Any Plan adopting a moving background plate prices its construction in the parameter table: for techniques where a moving plate IS the technique (the RSVP family form), the plate is a seamless tiled pan — repeat period = pan wrap, pan clock continuous across cycle wrap, and **tiles endpoint-periodic** (a tile whose end state differs from its start state steps at the join — T139 motion lens, Cycle 023). For a case-only stream technique (T137's form), the Plan prices a still plate — any background motion exceeds the technique.
114:6. **Parameterization depth (A9.6 — pattern promotion, §9 rule: the reusability finding recurred across T139 + T140 in Cycle 023, both lens-minimum at 78).** Every subject-shaping value is CONFIG data, not a code literal: vehicle/geometry branch data, background-layer geometry and growth/opacity coefficients (a background layer's coefficients are subject-shaping when a lens can price them against the subject — the T140 dust classification is resolved this way), and plate tile data. The config-census leg (A6) prices literals of these classes as HITs from Wave 21 onward; guard-config-census.md's classification text is amended in the same landing's guard sweep or carries the not-yet-amended note in the cycle record that first applies it.
120:3. **Motion-census Observe form (A10.3 — restates the Cycle-025 Observe form at instruction level).** §1 files a frame census at ≤2 s spacing across each item's transitions (seat-extracted, viewed firsthand by the seat), full SHA-256 per frame, with the motion findings stated per item: which instants carry motion, which are holds, and what changes between census states. Findings priced from a digest's prose alone, without the census, do not close Observe.
